as covid continues to surge in nevada  state leaders are starting to look at  whether recent mitigation efforts  have had an impact on the spread of  coronavirus kate houston has that report  it's been just over three weeks since  governor sisolak announced stay at home  2.0  the two-week initiative was put in place  to try to bring down the surging numbers  of kovid 19  in the state on a wednesday call with  nevada health response the team  addressed the drop in covid 19 cases  from this time last week  and whether or not that's a result of  recent mitigation efforts  caleb cage the nevada kovid 19 response  director  says it's important to look at these  numbers in terms of trends over time  there's always a two to three week lag  when a case is identified and  when we start reporting them there is  also a two to three week lag  for when we start to see mitigation  measures or when we could start to see  mitigation measures  take hold cage says they're unsure if  the decrease is due to a lower number of  people getting tested over the holidays  or from any changes in who went to get  tested it certainly does not  include any of the impacts that we may  or may not see  from thanksgiving so all of that is to  say yes  a decrease in a thousand cases is  positive but it is too early to  call this a trend or to even see what  the causes of that might be  with this in mind it may be a few more  weeks before we start to see the effects  of statewide mitigation efforts  including the recent statewide pause put  in place by governor sislak  kate houston 8 news now nevada health  response  announced updated cdc guidance on  quarantine periods new guidelines state  individuals can go back to normal  activities  after a seven day period if they tested  negative starting on day five of  quarantine  this also applies to individuals after  10 days if they are asymptomatic and  testing  is not available
 

